                "text": "**Analyzing Shape Alignment**\n\nI've been examining the task of aligning shapes, specifically focusing on how they move to achieve vertical or horizontal alignment. In the initial example (train 1), the red shape (2) and yellow shape (4) both moved to row indices that put them in alignment with the blue shape (1). The resulting configuration shows complete horizontal alignment across the involved shapes.\n\n\n**Investigating Alignment Logic**\n\nI'm now digging deeper into the alignment rules. The key seems to be aligning the *bottom* row of the shapes. Shapes with the same bottom row in the input will have the same bottom row in the output. The shapes that did move in the output were the ones whose bottom row did not match the lowest bottom row in the input set. I've begun to wonder about edge cases where multiple shapes have the same bottom row, but different heights.\n\n\n**Confirming Color Alignment**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. It's not *just* about the lowest bottom row. It's the bottom row of the *blue* shape (color 1) that dictates the alignment. I'm now certain all shapes align their bottoms to match the bottom row of the blue shape in each example.\n\n\n",
